 "founding_year": 2005,
 "history": "Y Combinator was founded in March 2005 in Cambridge, Massachusetts by Paul Graham, Jessica Livingston, Robert Morris and Trevor Blackwell, and ran its first Summer Founders Program that year. The batch model — a fixed cheque, a fixed programme and a Demo Day — was the innovation, and it moved to Mountain View, California soon after. Sam Altman was president 2014-2019, Geoff Ralston 2019-2022 and Garry Tan from January 2023. YC Continuity, the growth fund, launched in October 2015 and was wound back in March 2023 when YC scaled off late-stage investing. YC now runs four batches a year (from 2025) and states it has funded over 5,000 companies since 2005; its public directory listed 6,203 launched companies when this base was built.",
 "investment_concept": "YC invests a fixed amount in every company it accepts, on identical terms, and runs them through a three-month programme ending in Demo Day. It is an accelerator rather than a stage investor: selection and volume do the work that diligence and cheque-sizing do elsewhere.",
